    "summary": "Propaganda-satirical relief-print, made of black ink and hand-coloured with fibre-tipped pen on shell-coated hand-made paper. It shows two customers refusing to buy foreign goods from a seductive young female shop vendor, who is leaning across the counter dressed in an &#039;ao dai&#039; over white trousers and high heels. The vendor&#039;s emporium offers tempting imported commodities: &#039;ruoi mui&#039; (liquor), &#039;keo tag&#039; (sweets), &#039;xiga&#039; (cigars), &#039;thuoc la&#039; (cigarettes), &#039;biscuit&#039; (biscuits), &#039;cacao&#039; (chocolate) and &#039;do hop&#039; (preserved or canned food). Signed with an unidentified seal mark and the letters &quot;Khu&quot;, and inscribed.",
